s introduced distinctive Christological positions (as did the so-called tritheists or more mainstream “Nestorians” and miaphysites) that may throw light on Qurʾanic conundrums. The chapter advocates that Syriac literature of controversy constitutes a historical touchstone for discussions about possible Christian influences on the Qurʾān.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Debié, Muriel. “Les controverses miaphysites en Arabie et le Coran.” In <title level="m">Les controverses religieuses en syriaque</title>, edited by Flavia Ruani, 137–56. Études syriaques 13. Paris: Paul Geuthner, 2016.
